Hmm, could `console.log` truncation hide... no, raw: [] is definitive.

OH MY. I finally see it — look at the REPLICA fixture creation: `var f = ioFixture({ threads: [...] })` — then `f.comments = [...]`. But the suite ALSO has an EARLIER test ('replica of the mismatch test')... and the FIRST dbg suite ALSO declared `ioFixture`... The replica suite declares its OWN ioFixture INSIDE the suite callback ✓. And verdictComment is also declared there ✓.

Hmm — wait, is it possible that `verdictComment` assignment happens BEFORE ioFixture... no.

Let me be systematic: print Object.keys(f) and f.comments.length INSIDE getComments via a second closure variable. Actually — simplest hypothesis to check: the module's commentsTool passes `{workspace, repository, pullRequestId}` — my getComments signature `function ()` — no args — fine.

ANOTHER hypothesis: `_dbg.mb` — the mergeBackstop module was loaded with makeRequire mapping — mcpParseMod loaded ONCE. `githubTools(owner, name, io)` — normalizedIo(io) — b.getComments = f.io.getComments ✓ (function). commentsTool(b, owner, name): `if (!b.getComments) return null; return function (prNumber) { return mcpParse.payloadList(mcpParse.parseMcp(b.getComments({...}))); };` — calls b.getComments → my fixture function → raw...

Hmm — could there be TWO separate `f` objects — i.e. the ioFixture RETURNED a different object than the one whose getComments closed over? Look at my replica ioFixture:

```js
function ioFixture(opts) {
  var f = { threads: [], comments: [], existingIssues: [] };
  for (var k in (opts || {})) f[k] = opts[k];
  var rec = { posts: [], issues: [] };
  var io = { getThreads: ..., getComments: function () {...f...}, ... };
  return { io: io, rec: rec, fixture: f };
}
``

`f.comments = [...]` — the test sets it on the RETURNED `f` (r.fixture) — wait!! The test does:

```js
var f = ioFixture({ threads: [...] });
f.comments = [verdictComment(2, 1)];
``

ioFixture returns `{ io, rec, fixture: f }` — so the test's `f` variable is the WRAPPER `{io, rec, fixture}` — NOT the inner fixture object!! `f.comments = [...]` sets `comments` on the WRAPPER, not on `fixture.comments`!!! The closure reads `fixture.comments` (the inner `f`) which stays `[]`!!!

In the REAL test file, the ioFixture returns `{ io: io, rec: rec, fixture: f }` too — and existing tests set `f.fixture.threads[0].isResolved = true` (through fixture) — while the fixture INIT takes opts via `f[k] = opts[k]` INSIDE. So in the real test file, my new tests that do `f.comments = [...]` are BROKEN the same way — they must use `f.fixture.comments = [...]`.

That's the bug in MY TESTS. Fix: use `f.fixture.comments = [...]` in the new suite (and in the replica). Let me fix the three census tests.

